          %h2#properties Properties
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                In a cartesian closed category, the product functors
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ×
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \times -
                have
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoints"} right adjoints
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproducts"} coproducts
                is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributive+category"} distributive category
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
                of cartesian closed categories is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/minimal+logic"} minimal logic
                or the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/typed+lambda-calculus"} typed lambda-calculus
          %h3#inheritance_by_reflective_subcategories Inheritance by reflective subcategories
          %p
            In showing that a given category is cartesian closed, the following theorem is often useful (cf. A4.3.1 in the
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Elephant"} Elephant
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is cartesian closed, and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D\subseteq C
              is a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflective+subcategory"} reflective subcategory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L\colon C\to D
              preserves finite
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/products"} products
              if and only if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential+ideal"} exponential ideal
              (i.e.
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y\in D
              implies
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Y
                      %mi X
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y^X\in D
              for any
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\in C
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              preserves finite products, then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              is cartesian closed.
          %h3#exponentials_of_cartesian_closed_categories Exponentials of cartesian closed categories
          %p
            The following observation was taken from a post of Mike Shulman at
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/questions/104152/exponentials-in-functor-categories/104178#104178"} MathOverflow
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            is small and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒟
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
            is complete and cartesian closed, then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}^{\mathcal{C}}
            is also complete and cartesian closed. Completeness is clear since limits in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^C
            are computed pointwise. As for cartesian closure, we can compute exponentials in essentially the same way as for presheaves, motivated by
            = succeed "-enriched" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒟
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi G
                    %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi 𝒞
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  G^F(x) = \int_{y\in \mathcal{C}} \prod_{\mathcal{C}(x,y)} G(y)^{F(y)}.
          %p Then we can compute
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
